==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈbɪkə/}}
*: {{rhymes|en|ɪkə(r)}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|bikeren|t=to attack}}, from {{der|en|dum|bicken|t=to stab, thrust, attack}} {{suf|en||er|id2=verbal frequentative|pos2=frequentative suffix}}. Middle Dutch ''bicken'' is umtilately from {{der|en|gem-pro|*bikjaną}} (compare {{cog|ang|becca|t=pickax}}, {{cog|nl|bikken|t=to hack}}, {{cog|de|picken|t=to peck, pick at}}, {{cog|non|bikkja|t=to plunge into water}}), from {{der|en|ine-pro|*bʰeg-|t=to smash, break}}. Compare also {{cog|nds-de|bickern|t=to nibble, gnaw}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# To [[quarrel]] in a [[tiresome]], insulting manner.
#: ''They '''bickered''' about dinner every evening.''
#* {{rfdatek|en|Barrow}}
#*: petty things about which men cark and '''bicker'''
# To [[brawl]] or move [[tremulously]], quiver, shimmer (of a water stream, light, flame, etc.)
#* '''1886''', ''[[s:The Brook (Tennyson)|The Brook]]'', by [[w:Alfred, Lord Tennyson|Tennyson]]
#*: I come from haunts of coot and hern, / I make a sudden sally, / And sparkle out among the fern, / To '''bicker''' down a valley.
#* {{rfdatek|en|Thomson}}
#*: They [streamlets] '''bickered''' through the sunny shade.
# (of rain) To [[patter]].
# To skirmish; to [[exchange blows]]; to fight.
#* {{rfdatek|en|Holland}}
#*: Two eagles had a conflict, and '''bickered''' together.

